WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.780
<v Alice>What if the real danger with coding agents is the good output, not the bad?

2
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:11.317
<v Bob>Addy Osmani warns that most outputs will be well-written and mostly correct, but they won't demand a second look.

3
00:00:11.617 --> 00:00:14.946
<v Alice>So how do you keep thinking when agents do the work?

4
00:00:19.246 --> 00:00:29.982
<v Alice>Welcome to Build Wiz AI Show. Big AI ideas, made clear for builders. I'm Alice, and today I'm the one asking. Bob, what are we unpacking?

5
00:00:30.282 --> 00:00:41.750
<v Bob>I'm Bob! Today it's Addy Osmani's X article, "Critical thinking when agents do the work". He's clear up front: he's a big fan of these tools, and he's not arguing for less agentic workflows.

6
00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:45.983
<v Alice>So this isn't an anti-AI rant. What's his goal, then?

7
00:00:46.283 --> 00:00:57.495
<v Bob>In his words, to help you maintain critical thinking in a world where it's easy to offload anything to an agent. I'll add research on automation, and three guests bring two studies and a counterpoint.

8
00:00:57.795 --> 00:01:02.274
<v Alice>Start me at zero. Why is this harder now than before agents?

9
00:01:02.574 --> 00:01:17.437
<v Bob>Addy says the space between question and answer, where most of our thinking used to happen, is now measured in seconds. As models improve, there are fewer obvious errors, yet even with many agents running, you still only have one pair of eyes on the outputs.

10
00:01:17.737 --> 00:01:22.102
<v Alice>Fewer errors sounds good. So why don't we notice ourselves slipping?

11
00:01:22.402 --> 00:01:32.883
<v Bob>Because it feels nice. Fast, pleasant, often-correct answers are refreshing in a busy day, Addy says, and that's exactly why it's easy to relax your vigilance.

12
00:01:33.183 --> 00:01:37.279
<v Alice>Is that relaxing a known effect, or just a feeling we all have?

13
00:01:37.579 --> 00:01:52.407
<v Bob>It's well studied. Raja Parasuraman and Dietrich Manzey reviewed the research on automation complacency. It shows up when other tasks compete for your attention, it hits experts as well as novices, and it can't be overcome with simple practice.

14
00:01:52.707 --> 00:01:55.653
<v Alice>Experts too? Well, that's humbling.

15
00:01:55.953 --> 00:02:07.931
<v Bob>Picture a lifeguard at a pool where nobody ever struggles: by the afternoon, their eyes drift. A stream of well-written, mostly correct agent output is that quiet pool, and you're the lifeguard.

16
00:02:08.231 --> 00:02:11.560
<v Alice>So the better the agent gets, the quieter the pool.

17
00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:27.218
<v Bob>Exactly. Uwe Friedrichsen's post on the ironies of automation revisits Lisanne Bainbridge's 1983 paper. She wrote that even a highly motivated human can't keep effective visual attention on a source where very little happens for more than about half an hour.

18
00:02:27.518 --> 00:02:31.613
<v Alice>Half an hour? That's shorter than most of my agent sessions!

19
00:02:31.913 --> 00:02:50.283
<v Bob>So Addy describes the slide as a spectrum. At the top is cognitive offloading: delegating to the AI but keeping ownership of the result, which he does all day. In the middle is deferral, where the tool's output becomes your new input, and at the bottom, cognitive surrender: the AI's output quietly becomes your own.

20
00:02:50.583 --> 00:02:54.214
<v Alice>Surrender is a strong word. Where does it come from?

21
00:02:54.514 --> 00:03:08.541
<v Bob>He credits the term to Steven Shaw and Gideon Nave at Wharton. Think of it as GPS on a road trip: offloading is using the map and still knowing roughly where you are. Surrender is following the voice down a road you'd never have picked.

22
00:03:08.841 --> 00:03:11.759
<v Alice>And nobody notices the moment it happens?

23
00:03:12.059 --> 00:03:24.966
<v Bob>Rarely, he says, because each step is gradual and seems reasonable. You read the last answer well, so you skim the next one, then just scan the skeleton, until someone asks about a line and you can't explain it.

24
00:03:25.266 --> 00:03:28.944
<v Alice>Ouch. I have definitely scanned a skeleton or two.

25
00:03:29.244 --> 00:03:39.933
<v Bob>That, Addy says, is how cognitive debt accumulates. The codebase looks pristine and the tests pass, until you have to change it and realize that nobody understands it anymore.

26
00:03:40.233 --> 00:03:44.840
<v Alice>Is there data showing this drift is real? Maya, you read the study.

27
00:03:45.140 --> 00:04:07.294
<v Maya>Yes. Hao-Ping Lee and colleagues at Microsoft Research surveyed 319 knowledge workers for a CHI 2025 paper, and they shared 936 first-hand examples of using generative AI at work. The headline: higher confidence in GenAI is associated with less critical thinking, while higher self-confidence is associated with more.

28
00:04:07.594 --> 00:04:12.676
<v Alice>So trusting the tool more means checking less. Does the thinking just disappear?

29
00:04:12.976 --> 00:04:33.168
<v Maya>Not disappear, shift: the paper says GenAI shifts critical thinking toward information verification, response integration, and task stewardship. It's like going from cooking the meal to running the pass in a restaurant, where you still need to know what a good plate looks like. One caveat: the title itself says these are self-reported reductions.

30
00:04:33.468 --> 00:04:38.028
<v Alice>Thanks, Maya. Bob, so when is it fine to just hand it over?

31
00:04:38.328 --> 00:04:45.314
<v Bob>Addy's rule: take the handoff where being wrong is cheap, and keep the reasoning where others will have to deal with the consequences.

32
00:04:45.614 --> 00:04:50.348
<v Alice>So how does he stay at the top of that spectrum? He has a list of questions, right?

33
00:04:50.648 --> 00:05:02.917
<v Bob>He does, and he admits they look absurdly child-like. The first: did I formulate a hypothesis before asking? Before any non-trivial task, he writes down what he thinks the answer might be, even a bad guess.

34
00:05:03.217 --> 00:05:06.418
<v Alice>Why bother guessing if the agent's about to tell me anyway?

35
00:05:06.718 --> 00:05:26.880
<v Bob>Because once you've read a well-written response, he says, it's hard to imagine any other possibility. It's like guessing a price before you see the tag in a shop: once you've seen the tag, your own guess is gone, and your hypothesis is that guess. It also fits the Microsoft study Maya shared: higher self-confidence went with more critical thinking.

36
00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:31.368
<v Alice>Okay, I'm sold on guessing first. What's next on the list?

37
00:05:31.668 --> 00:05:45.475
<v Bob>Am I asking it to help me think, or to offload thinking, knowing both are fine? What would have to be true for this to be wrong? Then go test it, since a model's self-assessment of confidence is much less valuable than an actual test.

38
00:05:45.775 --> 00:05:48.949
<v Alice>Is there a question on that list that stings a little?

39
00:05:49.249 --> 00:06:06.201
<v Bob>This one: what am I no longer practicing? He's fine letting some routine debugging go, as long as it's intentional. Friedrichsen's post shows why it matters: Bainbridge warned that a formerly experienced operator who has been monitoring an automated process may now be an inexperienced one.

40
00:06:06.501 --> 00:06:16.047
<v Alice>This next part sounds familiar. In our short episode Verification as a habit, the point was that the bottleneck has shifted from coding to being sure you're right.

41
00:06:16.347 --> 00:06:31.175
<v Bob>And here it sits inside a bigger argument. Give an agent the means to verify its work, like end-to-end tests, property tests or real app interactions. Then it can change, test, read the output and try again before you've looked at anything.

42
00:06:31.475 --> 00:06:34.661
<v Alice>So I just watch the checks turn green and relax?

43
00:06:34.961 --> 00:06:51.659
<v Bob>That's the trap: you can surrender verification too. If the test is written with the same mistake as the feature, it'll pass, and a green check only conveys one property. Think of a smoke alarm: silence tells you there's no smoke, not that the oven is off, and a green check is that silence.

44
00:06:51.959 --> 00:06:54.522
<v Alice>So what do I ask when it goes green?

45
00:06:54.822 --> 00:07:10.381
<v Bob>What did it prove, and what didn't it? And define verified upfront, because after the fact, you'll rationalize any outcome. We heard a sharper cousin of this in our Claude Code Verification episode: if a change can modify the check that judges it, the check is broken.

46
00:07:10.681 --> 00:07:13.627
<v Alice>Is there a cheap trick here that actually works?

47
00:07:13.927 --> 00:07:21.819
<v Bob>One he swears by: ask "Did I miss anything?" at the end, and a frontier model will often come back with two or three more things.

48
00:07:22.119 --> 00:07:25.494
<v Alice>Did I miss anything? I'm stealing that one tonight.

49
00:07:25.794 --> 00:07:39.447
<v Bob>Then there's actually reading what's generated. Say an agent hands you a 600-line PR: the variable names look reasonable and the tests pass, so you approve it. But somewhere in the middle there's a subtle ordering change in a transaction boundary.

50
00:07:39.747 --> 00:07:42.416
<v Alice>But I reviewed it. Technically.

51
00:07:42.716 --> 00:07:51.664
<v Bob>Only for what you already knew you'd find, he says. Writing code and reviewing it are different skills, and at some point reviewing turns into rubber stamping.

52
00:07:51.964 --> 00:07:55.212
<v Alice>Is rubber stamping a new problem, or an old one?

53
00:07:55.512 --> 00:08:11.234
<v Bob>Very old. Friedrichsen quotes Bainbridge on the classic fix of making operators keep a log: unfortunately, people can write down numbers without noticing what they are. It's like initialling every page of a lease you haven't read, and an approved PR can be exactly that.

54
00:08:11.534 --> 00:08:13.969
<v Alice>So how do I read a diff properly?

55
00:08:14.269 --> 00:08:28.459
<v Bob>Read it as if the AI didn't write it, then look past it at what the agent decided: the defaults, what's out of scope, which requirement it reinterprets. Ask it to list its decisions and assumptions, then quiz yourself on a few lines at random.

56
00:08:28.759 --> 00:08:31.496
<v Alice>And what about the sheer volume of it all?

57
00:08:31.796 --> 00:08:45.904
<v Bob>He calls it the orchestration tax: the gap between what your agents generate and what you'd feel comfortable putting your name on. If finished work piles up faster than you can get through it, another session won't help: tighten the checks, or slow down.

58
00:08:46.204 --> 00:08:48.558
<v Alice>Give me his punchline on that one.

59
00:08:48.858 --> 00:08:53.766
<v Bob>When you're producing more than you can stand behind, you're no longer reviewing. You're hoping.

60
00:08:54.066 --> 00:08:59.868
<v Alice>Let's flip to the front end, the prompt. Why does he call prompting a critical thinking skill?

61
00:09:00.168 --> 00:09:15.077
<v Bob>Because your mental model now sits at the front and the end, not in the middle. In the past, if you made a non-specific request, someone would challenge it. An agent often won't stop you: it fills in what you left out and hands back something that looks done.

62
00:09:15.377 --> 00:09:19.108
<v Alice>So the agent is too polite to tell me my idea is fuzzy.

63
00:09:19.408 --> 00:09:33.331
<v Bob>Right. It's like ordering in a restaurant by saying "something nice": you'll get a plate, but the chef had to guess what you wanted. Addy calls those missing goals, constraints and rationale intent debt, and nobody else can fix that one for you.

64
00:09:33.631 --> 00:09:36.066
<v Alice>So what's the fix in practice?

65
00:09:36.366 --> 00:09:47.276
<v Bob>Write your own brief in your own words first: the audience, what's out of scope, and the definition of done. And when your second round of corrections hasn't got you there, stop tweaking and rewrite the prompt.

66
00:09:47.576 --> 00:09:51.591
<v Alice>And on the output side? Everything looks so polished now.

67
00:09:51.891 --> 00:10:08.379
<v Bob>That's his point: polish is no longer a useful signal. Friedrichsen says the same of AI errors, usually disguised in an overly self-confident presentation which makes them harder to spot. Addy's bar: if he wouldn't feel comfortable attributing the output to himself, he won't output it.

68
00:10:08.679 --> 00:10:16.060
<v Alice>Now the objection everyone's thinking: in a year or two, won't the model detect what I'd detect? Why keep the skill?

69
00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:28.884
<v Bob>Addy says you may be right on a lot of that detection, and he argues for pushing detection to agents himself. But someone is responsible for what ships, and how much to trust an agent for a given task is itself a judgment call.

70
00:10:29.184 --> 00:10:32.084
<v Alice>And how do you stay calibrated on that trust?

71
00:10:32.384 --> 00:10:47.222
<v Bob>By reviewing the work, because what the models get wrong keeps changing. He quotes Anthropic's own research, which calls it the paradox of supervision: "supervising Claude requires the very coding skills that may atrophy from AI overuse."

72
00:10:47.522 --> 00:10:50.312
<v Alice>That sounds like Bainbridge all over again.

73
00:10:50.612 --> 00:11:10.711
<v Bob>Friedrichsen calls it the expert as observer paradox: automating the process gives the operator a task that's only possible for someone who is in on-line control. Imagine a driving instructor who hasn't driven in years: the day the learner swerves, the instinct to grab the wheel still has to be there. When your agent gets it wrong, you're that instructor.

74
00:11:11.011 --> 00:11:16.256
<v Alice>Theo, you looked at the other side. Does AI actually wear skills down at work?

75
00:11:16.556 --> 00:11:34.031
<v Theo>It's more mixed than that. Erik Brynjolfsson, Danielle Li and Lindsey Raymond, in "Generative AI at Work" on arXiv, studied 5,172 customer support agents given an AI assistant. Productivity, measured by issues resolved per hour, rose 15 percent on average.

76
00:11:34.331 --> 00:11:36.639
<v Alice>And who gained the most from it?

77
00:11:36.939 --> 00:11:54.959
<v Theo>Less experienced and lower-skilled workers improved both speed and quality, while the most experienced saw small gains in speed and small declines in quality. They also found evidence that the assistant helped workers learn. Picture training wheels that also teach balance: the skill floor rises, which is a hopeful counter to the atrophy story.

78
00:11:55.259 --> 00:12:01.398
<v Alice>So novices climb, and experts slip a little. Bob, where does that leave accountability?

79
00:12:01.698 --> 00:12:12.910
<v Bob>Where Addy puts it: you may offload the work and much of the auditing, but not the accountability. What you're building, why it's worth it and who it impacts are questions a better model won't answer for you.

80
00:12:13.210 --> 00:12:17.817
<v Alice>Time to push back. Sam, you brought the skeptic's case.

81
00:12:18.117 --> 00:12:34.698
<v Sam>I did. Ethan Mollick argues on One Useful Thing that AI doesn't damage our brains, but unthinking use can damage our thinking. He reminds us that Plato thought writing would undermine our wisdom, and some people worried that not remembering phone numbers would make us dumber.

82
00:12:34.998 --> 00:12:36.807
<v Alice>So the panic is overblown?

83
00:12:37.107 --> 00:12:57.810
<v Sam>The panic, yes, but Mollick says it's how you use AI, rather than use of AI at all, that decides whether it helps or hurts. His Penn colleagues ran an experiment at a high school in Turkey: students using ChatGPT without guidance scored 17 percent worse on their final exam. A better tutor prompt eliminated that drop.

84
00:12:58.110 --> 00:13:01.149
<v Alice>So Addy and Mollick agree more than they disagree?

85
00:13:01.449 --> 00:13:13.125
<v Sam>On the fix, mostly. Mollick's rule is "think first, write first, meet first", and he warns that once you see AI's ideas, it becomes much harder to think outside those boundaries.

86
00:13:13.425 --> 00:13:25.539
<v Bob>Which is Addy's hypothesis-first habit under another name. But here's an honest worry for Addy's question list: Parasuraman and Manzey found that automation bias can't be prevented by training or instructions.

87
00:13:25.839 --> 00:13:29.178
<v Alice>Wait, so a list of good questions won't save me?

88
00:13:29.478 --> 00:13:45.966
<v Bob>Not willpower alone, in my view, so I'd lean on his set-up habits, like defining verified upfront, over trying to stay alert by force. Also, the Microsoft study is self-reported, and Addy's practices come from his own experience; to be fair, he says you don't need to do all this rigorously.

89
00:13:46.266 --> 00:13:50.617
<v Alice>Time for Builder's Corner. Bob, what's one thing to try this week?

90
00:13:50.917 --> 00:13:59.691
<v Bob>Addy's own closing exercise: before you ask the agent, write down what you think will happen. After it's done, write down one thing it didn't check.

91
00:13:59.991 --> 00:14:02.937
<v Alice>And how do I keep my own skills from fading?

92
00:14:03.237 --> 00:14:15.634
<v Bob>Once a week, write a bit of code without an agent. Once a month, write down what you've stopped doing and decide which of those you're okay not doing. Then ask his old question: how lost would you be if AI didn't exist?

93
00:14:15.934 --> 00:14:22.293
<v Alice>Let's land it. My first takeaway: know where you are on the spectrum, offloading or surrender.

94
00:14:22.593 --> 00:14:27.710
<v Bob>Have a hypothesis before you ask, and when a check goes green, ask what it didn't check.

95
00:14:28.010 --> 00:14:36.030
<v Alice>From our guests: trusting the tool more tends to mean thinking less, and how you use AI matters more than whether you use it.

96
00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:40.390
<v Bob>And set a bar: if you wouldn't put your name on it, it doesn't go out.

97
00:14:40.690 --> 00:14:42.916
<v Alice>What's still open for you, Bob?

98
00:14:43.216 --> 00:14:50.428
<v Bob>My open question: do better models make the quiet pool even quieter, with fewer obvious errors to keep us alert?

99
00:14:50.728 --> 00:14:56.066
<v Alice>And mine: how do newcomers build judgment if they rarely do the work by hand?

100
00:14:56.366 --> 00:15:06.080
<v Bob>Bainbridge saw that one coming: today's monitors are riding on skills that later generations of operators can't be expected to have. I'd bet that's the next big debate for builders.

101
00:15:06.380 --> 00:15:17.117
<v Alice>Thanks to Addy Osmani for his X article, to Raja Parasuraman and Dietrich Manzey for their review on complacency, and to Uwe Friedrichsen on the ironies of automation.

102
00:15:17.417 --> 00:15:28.079
<v Bob>And thanks to Hao-Ping Lee and colleagues at Microsoft Research, Ethan Mollick on One Useful Thing, and Erik Brynjolfsson, Danielle Li and Lindsey Raymond for "Generative AI at Work".

103
00:15:28.379 --> 00:15:36.271
<v Alice>The voices are AI, and every claim was checked against those sources. Before your next prompt, write down your guess.

104
00:15:36.571 --> 00:15:41.304
<v Bob>That's all for today on Build Wiz AI Show. Thanks for listening, and see you next time.
